{0 Binary encoding}

{!Eon_edn} reads text, but a parsed {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.value} can also
be written to and read back from a compact binary form. The default codec
is {!Eon_edn.Binary}; its pieces are deliberately small and separate so you
can swap the representation without touching the data model or its
consumers. The companion example is
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_edn/examples/book/codec_example.ml}[codec_example.ml]}.

{1 Encoding and decoding}

{!Eon_edn.Binary.Encode.encode} turns a value into an
{!Eon_edn.Edn_bytes.t}; {!Eon_edn.Binary.Decode.decode} turns it back.

{[
  let v =
    VMap [ (VKeyword "name", VString "Crab"); (VKeyword "pos", VVector [ VNumber 1.; VNumber 2. ]) ]
  in
  let bytes = Binary.Encode.encode v in
  assert (Binary.Decode.decode bytes = v)
]}

{!Eon_edn.Edn_bytes.t} is [private string]: it costs nothing at runtime and
reads as a string through a coercion [(bytes :> string)], but it can only
be built with {!Eon_edn.Edn_bytes.of_string}. That keeps encoded binary and
EDN text, both strings, from being handed to the wrong function — never
pass an [Edn_bytes.t] to {!Eon_edn.Edn_parser}.

The format keeps the AST, not the source text: list vs vector and string vs
keyword stay distinct, map entry order is preserved, and floats survive
bit-for-bit (NaN payloads and [-0.0] included). Comments, whitespace and
numeric spelling are gone, as they are after parsing.

{1 Tags and middleware}

[Encode] writes [VTagged] and [VMeta] nodes as plain data and never runs
middleware. [Decode] behaves like the text parser: for each tagged or meta
node it performs {!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Tag} or
{!Eon_edn.Edn_effects.Meta}, bottom-up, and never consults the tag
registry itself. So run it inside
{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.with_middleware}, the same handler stack
{!Eon_edn.Edn_middleware.run_with_middleware} uses for text:

{[
  let bytes = Binary.Encode.encode (VTagged ("shout", VString "hi")) in
  let v = M.with_middleware (fun () -> Binary.Decode.decode bytes) in
  assert (v = VString "HI")
]}

Because the middleware is the same, text and binary resolve tags
identically. [~resolve:false] makes that useful for precompiling: the
registry is not consulted, so every tag comes back as [VTagged] and the
compiled tree depends only on the text. Tag handlers then run when the
binary is loaded, not when it is built.

{[
  let tree = M.with_middleware ~resolve:false parse in
  assert (tree = VTagged ("shout", VString "x"));
  let bytes = Binary.Encode.encode tree in
  let from_binary = M.with_middleware (fun () -> Binary.Decode.decode bytes) in
  let from_text = M.run_with_middleware (fun () -> Eon_edn.Edn_parser.value ()) text in
  assert (from_binary = from_text)
]}

Allowlists apply to binary input too:

{[
  match
    M.with_middleware ~handlers:[ M.strict_tags [ "shout" ] ] (fun () ->
        Binary.Decode.decode bytes)
  with
  | exception Failure _ -> ()
  | _ -> assert false
]}

{1 Untrusted input}

Binary input may be hostile (mod files, network data). [Decode] raises
only [Failure] — never any other exception — on bad magic or version, an
unknown tag byte, truncated input, trailing bytes, a length or count larger
than the remaining input, or a limit violation. The limits come from
{!Eon_edn.Binary.LIMITS}: [max_size] (checked once, up front) and
[max_depth] (the top-level value is depth 0; each container, tagged or meta
value nests one deeper). {!Eon_edn.Binary.Default_limits} is 16 MiB and 256.
For other bounds, apply {!Eon_edn.Binary.Make}:

{[
  module Small = Eon_edn.Binary.Make (struct
    let max_size = 1024
    let max_depth = 8
  end)
]}

Limits are decode-side only: the encoder ignores them, and an encoder and
decoder pair by {i format} alone, so a limits mismatch between them is your
responsibility.

{1 Writing your own codec}

A codec is two module types, {!Eon_edn.ENCODE} and {!Eon_edn.DECODE}, so a
sender can depend on only one half. Each declares an abstract [format]; a
consumer needing both requires [DECODE with type format = ENCODE.format],
which makes the compiler reject a mismatched pair.

{[
  module Round_trip (E : Eon_edn.ENCODE) (D : Eon_edn.DECODE with type format = E.format) =
  struct
    let go v = D.decode (E.encode v)
  end

  module Binary_round_trip = Round_trip (Binary.Encode) (Binary.Decode)
]}

The [format] type has no runtime representation. It proves that both
modules claim the same format, not that their implementations agree — test
that with a round-trip property. Selection is at compile time, with no
registry or runtime dispatch. A custom [DECODE] should follow the same
contract: perform [Tag]/[Meta] for tagged and meta nodes and raise
[Failure] on bad input.

{1 Wire format}

A document is the magic bytes ["EDNB"], one version byte (currently [1]),
and a single value. Every value starts with one tag byte; lengths and
counts are unsigned LEB128 varints; numbers are IEEE-754 float64,
little-endian.

{v
0x00 nil              0x05 list    count, elements      0x0A tagged  name, value
0x01 false            0x06 vector  count, elements      0x0B meta    meta, value
0x02 true             0x07 map     pair count, k v ...
0x03 number float64   0x08 symbol  length, bytes
0x04 string           0x09 keyword length, bytes
     length, UTF-8 bytes
v}
